{"status": "ok", "message-type": "work-list", "message-version": "1.0.0", "message": {"facets": {}, "total-results": 3477973, "items": [{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 7]], "date-time": "2026-08-07T15:09:01Z", "timestamp": 1786115341736, "version": "build-2736575974"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 25]]}, "group-title": "Social Sciences", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "MDPI AG", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 25]], "date-time": "2026-06-25T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1782345600000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "accepted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 23]]}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Social and behavioral research often requires the examination of policy shocks, information interventions, platform-mediated attention, and governance feedback. However, direct experiments on real populations are frequently constrained by ethical risks, intervention costs, and limited repeatability. This study proposes Multi-Agent Social Simulation (MASS), a protocolized form of LLM-driven agent-based modeling designed as a quantitative research method for low-risk, repeatable, and auditable pre-experimental simulation. MASS embeds large language models into an agent-based framework through role-cluster modeling, round-based scheduling, local information boundaries, exogenous rule tables, structured outputs, harness checks, reason-action logs, and replication manifests.\nThe method is evaluated through three empirically referenced cases: the New Jersey\u2013Pennsylvania minimum wage natural experiment, the 2016 UK Brexit digital campaigning context, and the 2023 Zibo barbecue tourism attention event. The results show that protocolized LLM-driven ABM can generate analyzable and empirically assessable outputs across policy shocks, information interventions, and public-opinion dynamics. The strongest evidence appears in rule-shock identification, the reduction of undecided shares under targeting conditions, and multi-agent public-information interaction with mechanism-chain consistency among governance response, sentiment, and behavioral intention.\nMASS should not be treated as a substitute for real-world experiments or causal inference. Rather, it should be understood as a pre-experimental simulation method for mechanism rehearsal, risk identification, counterfactual comparison, and research-design preparation.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.20944/preprints202606.1832.v1", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 26]], "date-time": "2026-06-26T00:28:49Z", "timestamp": 1782433729000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Multi-Agent Social Simulation: Protocolizing LLM-Driven Agent-Based Modeling as a Quantitative Research Method"], "prefix": "10.20944", "author": [{"given": "Xiaoli", "family": "Hu", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Yang", "family": "Shen", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "1968", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 6]], "date-time": "2026-08-06T02:13:25Z", "timestamp": 1785982405000}, "score": 32.67114, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.preprints.org/manuscript/202606.1832/v1"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 25]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202606.1832.v1", "relation": {"is-preprint-of": [{"id-type": "doi", "id": "10.3390/ai7080279", "asserted-by": "subject"}]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 25]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 5, 21]], "date-time": "2026-05-21T21:25:20Z", "timestamp": 1779398720996, "version": "3.53.1"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Royal Society of Chemistry (RSC)", "content-domain": {"domain": []}, "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2025, 12, 7]]}, "DOI